00:00:10.490 --> 00:00:13.450
This is Indiana, and this is Scotland.

00:00:13.450 --> 00:00:18.340
Both have a similar number of inhabitants,
a similar size, and a similar population density.

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:22.369
But here’s Indiana’s public transportation
system, and here’s Scotland’s.

00:00:22.369 --> 00:00:26.460
You want to get to Cupar, a town of 9,000
30 miles from the capital?

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:30.990
That’ll take you 55 minutes on a train that
leaves every 30 minutes or an hour and 40

00:00:30.990 --> 00:00:33.000
minutes on a bus that leaves every 40.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:37.110
You want to get to Anderson, a town of 50,000
30 miles from Indiana’s capital?

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:38.850
Well, you’re out of luck.

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:40.910
The only option is the car.

00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:45.720
Antiquated technology, safety concerns, crumbling
infrastructure, and nonexistence—it’s

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:50.760
not hard to argue that the US public transportation
network is just not good.

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:56.280
Vast swaths of the US have no option but to
drive because the alternative just is not

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:57.280
there.

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:01.540
This has consequences on the environment,
on economic mobility, on where people live,

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:06.950
the consequences of America’s lack of solid
public transportation almost defines American

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:07.950
culture.

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:09.420
But it wasn’t always like this.

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:14.310
The United States once had the best public
transportation system in the world.

00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:19.810
It was a the admiration of countries worldwide
and an essential factor allowing for the successful

00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:21.560
western expansion of the country.

00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:24.130
It all started with this—the horsecar.

00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:28.850
Now, there were urban transportation systems
before these horse drawn trams came along,

00:01:28.850 --> 00:01:31.560
but they weren't cheap and they weren’t
fast.

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:35.170
Roads generally weren’t paved and there
just wasn’t the economic demand for high

00:01:35.170 --> 00:01:39.619
frequency service because these carriages
were rarely faster than walking.

00:01:39.619 --> 00:01:44.200
But on rails, these horsecars were fast and
one horse could pull a full load of passengers

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:45.580
thanks to the rails.

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:50.380
In its heyday, there were over 6,000 miles
of horsecar lines in the US.

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:55.509
In comparison, the combined mileage of every
tram, subway, light rail, and commuter rail

00:01:55.509 --> 00:01:58.729
system in the US nowadays is 5,416.

00:01:58.729 --> 00:02:03.680
In 1880, 50 million people lived in the US.

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:06.590
Today, over 320 million.

00:02:06.590 --> 00:02:10.489
Around the turn of the century, many of those
horsecar systems were electrified.

00:02:10.489 --> 00:02:14.549
There were then 11,000 miles of streetcar
track nationwide.

00:02:14.549 --> 00:02:16.950
The systems were absolutely everywhere.

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:21.389
Even tiny towns like Bangor, Maine and Berlin,
New Hampshire had streetcars.

00:02:21.389 --> 00:02:22.499
So what happened?

00:02:22.499 --> 00:02:27.590
How did the US go from having 11,000 miles
of streetcar to 200?

00:02:27.590 --> 00:02:32.230
How did the US go from having solid public
transportation in towns big and small across

00:02:32.230 --> 00:02:34.359
the country to how it is today?

00:02:34.359 --> 00:02:38.499
The decline of the streetcar began just after
the turn of the century.

00:02:38.499 --> 00:02:40.499
That was when the automobile came around.

00:02:40.499 --> 00:02:45.480
By 1920, the car was starting to get to an
attainable price-point for the everyday individual.

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:50.529
That was the real threat for the streetcar—not
cars, but economical cars.

00:02:50.529 --> 00:02:55.049
The streetcar received another blow in 1929—the
great depression.

00:02:55.049 --> 00:02:58.959
There were fewer people with jobs which meant
fewer people who needed to commute and fewer

00:02:58.959 --> 00:03:04.329
people who had the money to pay for transport
so many lines were just not profitable anymore

00:03:04.329 --> 00:03:05.329
and closed.

00:03:05.329 --> 00:03:09.739
But then the streetcar received a stay of
execution—World War Two.

00:03:09.739 --> 00:03:15.219
You see, during World War Two, the US had
the lowest unemployment rate in history—as

00:03:15.219 --> 00:03:17.159
low as 1.2%.

00:03:17.159 --> 00:03:21.569
There were tons of factory jobs to support
the war so practically everyone who wanted

00:03:21.569 --> 00:03:23.319
a job had a job.

00:03:23.319 --> 00:03:27.439
That meant there were tons more people now
going to and from work, and, even better for

00:03:27.439 --> 00:03:32.659
the streetcar, there were rations going on
on rubber and gas which diminished the popularity

00:03:32.659 --> 00:03:33.659
of the car.

00:03:33.659 --> 00:03:36.040
But something else was going on through all
of that.

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:37.169
Something more sinister.

00:03:37.169 --> 00:03:41.859
Sometime in the 1920s, automobile technology
became advanced enough that the bus became

00:03:41.859 --> 00:03:44.260
cheaper to operate than the streetcar.

00:03:44.260 --> 00:03:48.629
Streetcars cost very little to power, but
they do require a lot of infrastructure from

00:03:48.629 --> 00:03:50.629
overhead lines to track.

00:03:50.629 --> 00:03:54.120
Buses were more flexible and required almost
no infrastructure.

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:59.430
And the bus had some powerful friends, the
automobile companies, or more specifically,

00:03:59.430 --> 00:04:00.469
General Motors.

00:04:00.469 --> 00:04:04.719
General Motors went and bought dozens of small
streetcar companies across the nation and

00:04:04.719 --> 00:04:06.159
turned them into bus companies.

00:04:06.159 --> 00:04:11.019
They removed hundreds of miles of track across
the US and supported other companies doing

00:04:11.019 --> 00:04:14.389
the same, but its not like they didn’t have
a good reason to do this.

00:04:14.389 --> 00:04:17.669
These streetcars were not economically advantageous.

00:04:17.669 --> 00:04:22.310
Buses were faster, cheaper, and at the time,
they were the modern and fresh transportation

00:04:22.310 --> 00:04:24.000
method that the public wanted.

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:28.300
Nearly every streetcar system nationwide was
replaced with a bus system.

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:32.510
In addition, the streetcar companies were
almost all commercial so if and when they

00:04:32.510 --> 00:04:36.710
failed, many local governments set up public,
subsidized bus companies.

00:04:36.710 --> 00:04:41.520
So that’s how transportation got bad, but
why did it stay bad?

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:43.800
Well, mostly because of the car.

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:45.940
America is the country of the car.

00:04:45.940 --> 00:04:49.280
It grew up as the car grew up and so its cities
were built for cars.

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:54.650
Think Dallas, Phoenix, Los Angeles—you can’t
survive in these cities without a car.

00:04:54.650 --> 00:04:59.020
Remember, the United States is centered around
the idea of personal freedom.

00:04:59.020 --> 00:05:04.120
With a car, you can go anywhere at anytime,
so politically, cars have historically been

00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:07.120
associated with the idea of personal freedom.

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:11.530
Just like the Republican party votes to have
strong national defense, allow gun ownership,

00:05:11.530 --> 00:05:15.469
and preserve small government in order to
promote personal freedom, they have always

00:05:15.469 --> 00:05:18.409
worked to promote the usage and ownership
of cars.

00:05:18.409 --> 00:05:23.139
This means they often voted in favor of subsidies
helping the auto industry, most often in the

00:05:23.139 --> 00:05:26.650
form of indirect subsidies lowering the cost
of gas.

00:05:26.650 --> 00:05:31.490
Now, that was fine when cities were small,
highways were new, gas was cheap, and climate

00:05:31.490 --> 00:05:35.159
change wasn’t even a concept, but that’s
not the case anymore.

00:05:35.159 --> 00:05:40.180
Cities are just of a size where they literally
cannot support their entire population driving.

00:05:40.180 --> 00:05:46.259
You can’t fit more road infrastructure in
many cites, but you can fit more public transportation.

00:05:46.259 --> 00:05:50.539
Cars were available to the common American
much earlier than the common European, so

00:05:50.539 --> 00:05:56.680
the US set road policies early that allowed
for large, smooth, well-functioning roads.

00:05:56.680 --> 00:06:02.110
While the US was building its magnificent
roads, Europe was building their public transportation

00:06:02.110 --> 00:06:03.110
systems.

00:06:03.110 --> 00:06:06.139
The high car usage in the US even has to do
with zoning.

00:06:06.139 --> 00:06:10.940
You see, European cities tend to have less
strict zoning laws which allow for businesses

00:06:10.940 --> 00:06:12.949
and housing to intermingle.

00:06:12.949 --> 00:06:16.030
The US zones its cities much more strictly.

00:06:16.030 --> 00:06:20.180
Houses are next to houses and businesses are
next to businesses which means that the distances

00:06:20.180 --> 00:06:23.960
between houses and shops in the US is much
greater.

00:06:23.960 --> 00:06:27.099
Therefore, Americans have to go further more
often.

00:06:27.099 --> 00:06:31.280
The most demonstrative fact is how the two
places approach parking.

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:36.080
In the US, zoning laws specify a minimum number
of parking spaces per building.

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:40.490
In Europe, the laws specify a maximum number
of parking spaces.

00:06:40.490 --> 00:06:45.719
The three cities with the three lowest car-ownership
rates in the US all have something in common.

00:06:45.719 --> 00:06:51.949
Boston, New York, and DC, are all old, rather
compact cities with decent public transportation

00:06:51.949 --> 00:06:52.949
systems.

00:06:52.949 --> 00:06:57.430
Since they were cities before the car, they’re
built much more like the European cities that

00:06:57.430 --> 00:07:00.509
have such good public transportation systems
today.

00:07:00.509 --> 00:07:05.499
Simplified, public transportation gets worse
as you go further west since western cities

00:07:05.499 --> 00:07:06.499
are newer.

00:07:06.499 --> 00:07:11.710
But here’s the most important sentence of
this entire video: access to transportation

00:07:11.710 --> 00:07:16.779
is the single most important factor in an
individual’s ability to escape poverty.

00:07:16.779 --> 00:07:21.919
That is not a subjective claim, that is a
fact that emerged from a Harvard study.

00:07:21.919 --> 00:07:26.930
Someone who lives right by a subway stop is
astronomically more likely to find a high-paying

00:07:26.930 --> 00:07:30.539
job than someone who doesn’t have a way
to get around.

00:07:30.539 --> 00:07:34.680
Individuals in poverty generally live in poor
neighborhoods with few job opportunities,

00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:40.020
but with reliable, accessible, and inexpensive
public transportation these individuals can

00:07:40.020 --> 00:07:43.270
get all across their city to where the jobs
are.

00:07:43.270 --> 00:07:48.160
So, a good way to evaluate the effectiveness
of a public transportation system is by how

00:07:48.160 --> 00:07:49.910
well it serves the poor.

00:07:49.910 --> 00:07:52.409
DC, for example, does a good job of this.

00:07:52.409 --> 00:07:56.400
The poorest neighborhoods have the greatest
proportion of their residents within a 10-minute

00:07:56.400 --> 00:08:01.289
walk of a metro station while the richest
neighborhoods have the smallest proportion.

00:08:01.289 --> 00:08:06.059
Hand-in-hand with their move back into the
cities, millennials are shunning cars.

00:08:06.059 --> 00:08:11.319
Car ownership among young people is at historic
lows and the urban youth is relying more and

00:08:11.319 --> 00:08:13.349
more on public transport.

00:08:13.349 --> 00:08:18.490
Some cities like, Portland, Kansas City, Detroit,
and DC are turning back to streetcars.

00:08:18.490 --> 00:08:22.990
Done right, streetcars can drive huge increases
in economic development.

00:08:22.990 --> 00:08:28.539
They’re more of a symbol of modernization
that entices residents, developers, and businesses

00:08:28.539 --> 00:08:29.539
to areas.

00:08:29.539 --> 00:08:36.110
Portland, for example, has had an estimated
$5 billion in extra economic development thanks

00:08:36.110 --> 00:08:37.200
to its streetcar.

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:41.959
New streetcar systems are being built all
across the US in cities like Milwaukee and

00:08:41.959 --> 00:08:46.740
Oklahoma city since they’re finally making
money again—not from their fares, but from

00:08:46.740 --> 00:08:49.149
the jobs brought by their existence.

00:08:49.149 --> 00:08:53.490
People didn’t want them a century ago, but
streetcars finally make sense again.

00:08:53.490 --> 00:08:57.699
Public transportation is instrumentally important
to the success of cities.

00:08:57.699 --> 00:09:02.120
You can almost be sure that a good city will
have good public transportation and a bad

00:09:02.120 --> 00:09:04.940
city will have bad public transportation.

00:09:04.940 --> 00:09:10.279
Public transportation increases economic mobility,
decreases carbon footprints, and increases

00:09:10.279 --> 00:09:15.150
economic development so the only question
is, why not build more of it?

